      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the number of ordered pairs of integers \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(1 \\le x < y \\le 100\\) and \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number, we start by examining the properties of complex numbers and their powers.\n\nThe imaginary unit \\(i\\) is defined by the property that \\(i^2 = -1\\). The powers of \\(i\\) cycle every four values as follows:\n\\[\ni^1 = i, \\quad i^2 = -1, \\quad i^3 = -i, \\quad i^4 = 1,\n\\]\nand this pattern repeats. Therefore, for any integer \\(n\\), we have:\n\\[\ni^n = i^{n \\mod 4}.\n\\]\nThus, \\(i^x\\) and \\(i^y\\) will each be \\(i^x\\), \\(i^x\\), \\(i^{x+1}\\), or \\(i^{x+2}\\), and similarly for \\(i^y\\).\n\nWe need \\(i^x + i^y\\) to be real. For the sum of two complex numbers to be real, the imaginary parts must cancel each other out. Specifically, we need:\n\\[\ni^x + i^y = k,\n\\]\nwhere \\(k\\) is a real number. This occurs when:\n\\[\ni^{x+1} + i^{x+2} = k.\n\\]\n\nLet's explore the values of \\(i^{x+1}\\) and \\(i^{x+2}\\):\n\\[\ni^{x+1} = i(1)^x \\cdot i^1 = i \\cdot i = -i,\n\\]\n\\[\ni^{x+2} = i(1)^x \\cdot i^2 = i \\cdot (-1) = -i.\n\\]\n\nThus, we simplify:\n\\[\ni^{x+1} = -i,\n\\]\n\\[\ni^{x+2} = -i.\n\\]\n\nIt follows that:\n\\[\ni^{x+1} + i^{x+2} = -i + (-i) = -2i.\n\\]\n\nFor the sum \\(i^x + i^y\\) to be real, \\(2i\\) must equal some real number. However, \\(2i\\) is purely imaginary and cannot be expressed as a real number by combining with a complex number.\n\nSince \\(i^x + i^y\\) cannot be real unless it is zero (which is not the case here), there are no valid pairs \\((x, y)\\) that make \\(i^x + i^y\\) real.\n\nTherefore, the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(1 \\le x < y \\le 100\\) and \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
